-- =============================================================================
-- A group of ONE clause is checked as a clause ("This clause does not cover all
-- cases"), at the clause. Where that check gives up, the clause keeps the
-- warning it had before the check existed, the one for a CONSIDER, now at the
-- clause rather than at no location. Below: a clause with a number, which
-- the check reads (since round 2 item 4 of the review); a clause with more
-- than 64 missing clauses, where the check gives up; a control under that
-- cap; the same clause over the cap with a number in it, which the check
-- answers with a shorter list; and two clauses over the cap, the second
-- reported as never used (review of legalese/l4-ide#545; probes m01, p02,
-- p02b).
-- =============================================================================

-- A number in a pattern is one value of its input, and the input has no end
-- of other values: the clause for `1` with no second input, and the clause
-- for every other number, are both still needed (and are the same two cases
-- the CONSIDER warning used to list for the second input, and gave up on
-- for the first).
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      m IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `one clause with a literal` 1 (JUST x) IS x

DECLARE Big IS ONE OF B01, B02, B03, B04, B05, B06, B07, B08, B09, B10, B11, B12, B13, B14, B15, B16, B17, B18, B19, B20, B21, B22, B23, B24, B25

-- More than 64 missing clauses (three inputs of 26 values): the check gives
-- up, so the CONSIDER warnings, one per input, are kept.
GIVEN x IS A MAYBE Big
      y IS A MAYBE Big
      z IS A MAYBE Big
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `one clause three columns` (JUST B01) (JUST B01) (JUST B01) IS 1

-- Control: two such inputs leave 50 missing clauses, which the check lists.
GIVEN x IS A MAYBE Big
      y IS A MAYBE Big
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `one clause two columns` (JUST B01) (JUST B01) IS 1

-- The three inputs again, with a number before them: the exact list is over
-- the cap again, so the check lists instead what the clauses without a
-- number leave open. There are none, so everything is open, and that is one
-- clause, which is listed; the CONSIDER warnings are withdrawn.
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      x IS A MAYBE Big
      y IS A MAYBE Big
      z IS A MAYBE Big
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `one clause four columns` 1 (JUST B01) (JUST B01) (JUST B01) IS 1

-- Two clauses over the cap: no missing-clause warning, and the second
-- clause, which repeats the first, is still reported as never used.
GIVEN x IS A MAYBE Big
      y IS A MAYBE Big
      z IS A MAYBE Big
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `two clauses three columns` (JUST B01) (JUST B01) (JUST B01) IS 1
DECIDE `two clauses three columns` (JUST B01) (JUST B01) (JUST B01) IS 2

#EVAL `one clause with a literal` 1 (JUST 5)           -- 5
#EVAL `one clause three columns` (JUST B01) (JUST B01) (JUST B01) -- 1
#EVAL `one clause two columns` (JUST B01) (JUST B01)    -- 1
#EVAL `one clause four columns` 1 (JUST B01) (JUST B01) (JUST B01) -- 1
#EVAL `two clauses three columns` (JUST B01) (JUST B01) (JUST B01) -- 1
